\chapter{Informational Cosmology and Sovereign Architecture}

\section{Motivation and Historical Context}

The development of sovereign-class simulation engines arises from the
need to unify multi-domain physical, biological, and cosmological
systems under a single invariant-governed architecture. Traditional
simulation paradigms treat molecular dynamics, tensor-field physics, and
neural systems as disjoint domains. Sovereign architecture reframes these
domains as manifestations of a deeper informational cosmology.

\section{Sovereign Invariants and Engine Classes}

Sovereign engines are defined by invariant-governed evolution:
\begin{equation}
  \mathcal{R}_{\text{sovereign}}
  =
  R_1 \land R_2 \land \cdots \land R_n,
\end{equation}
where each $R_i$ is a domain-specific invariant (thermodynamic,
geometric, metabolic, or proteostatic). Engine classes are categorized by
the number of domains unified under a single invariant stack.

\section{Multi-Scale State-Space Philosophy}

The sovereign framework models systems using a multi-scale state vector:
\begin{equation}
  \Psi(x,t)
  =
  \big(
    \Psi_{\text{molecular}},
    \Psi_{\text{tensor}},
    \Psi_{\text{metabolic}},
    \Psi_{\text{network}}
  \big),
\end{equation}
with each component evolving under shared invariants and cross-domain
constraints.

